From: spycat88 <spycat88@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dsi/phy: leave the 7nm PLL alone while the PHY is
 suspended

The SM8750 and Eliza display clock controllers give the DSI byte and pixel
RCGs CLK_OPS_PARENT_ENABLE. When the PHY registers its clocks at probe,
the clock core adopts those RCGs as orphans and, because of the flag,
prepares and enables the PHY PLL around the reparent. The PHY is not
powered yet, so the PLL never locks and prepare fails; the core does not
check, and then disables and unprepares a parent it never enabled. On the
Retroid Pocket Duo every boot logged, for both PLL outputs:

  DSI PLL(0) lock failed, status=0x00000000
  PLL(0) lock failed
  dsi0_phy_pll_out_dsiclk already disabled
  WARNING: drivers/clk/clk.c:1257 at clk_core_disable+0x298/0x2e0
  ...
   __clk_set_parent_after+0xb0/0x118
   clk_core_reparent_orphans_nolock+0x88/0xd0
   ...
   dsi_phy_driver_probe+0x2f4/0x348
  dsi0_phy_pll_out_dsiclk already unprepared

Report success without touching the hardware while the PHY is runtime
suspended, and skip the matching unprepare. msm_dsi_phy_enable() resumes
the PHY before the DSI host prepares its link clocks, so every real use
still runs the full sequence; a prepare that would have reached a
suspended PHY could not lock it anyway.
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/phy/dsi_phy_7nm.c | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/phy/dsi_phy_7nm.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/phy/dsi_phy_7nm.c
@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@
 #include <linux/clk.h>
 #include <linux/clk-provider.h>
 #include <linux/iopoll.h>
+#include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
 
 #include "dsi_phy.h"
 #include "dsi.xml.h"
@@ -496,6 +497,17 @@
 	struct dsi_pll_7nm *pll_7nm = to_pll_7nm(hw);
 	int rc;
 
+	/*
+	 * A display clock controller whose byte and pixel sources carry
+	 * CLK_OPS_PARENT_ENABLE prepares this PLL when the clock core adopts
+	 * them as orphans, inside devm_of_clk_add_hw_provider() at PHY probe.
+	 * The PHY is not powered then: the PLL cannot lock, and the core goes
+	 * on to disable and unprepare a parent it never enabled. Leave the
+	 * hardware alone until msm_dsi_phy_enable() has resumed the PHY.
+	 */
+	if (pm_runtime_suspended(&pll_7nm->phy->pdev->dev))
+		return 0;
+
 	dsi_pll_enable_pll_bias(pll_7nm);
 	if (pll_7nm->slave)
 		dsi_pll_enable_pll_bias(pll_7nm->slave);
@@ -549,6 +561,11 @@
 {
 	struct dsi_pll_7nm *pll_7nm = to_pll_7nm(hw);
 
+	/* Prepare left a suspended PHY alone; so does this */
+	if (!pll_7nm->phy->pll_on &&
+	    pm_runtime_suspended(&pll_7nm->phy->pdev->dev))
+		return;
+
 	/*
 	 * To avoid any stray glitches while abruptly powering down the PLL
 	 * make sure to gate the clock using the clock enable bit before
